```console
$ ketch --help
ketch installs command-line tools and apps from GitHub releases on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
No taps, no formulae, no build step — it downloads what the project already ships.

Usage: ketch[EXE] [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  install      Install one or more packages [alias: i]
  uninstall    Remove installed packages [aliases: remove, rm]
  list         Show installed packages [alias: ls]
  outdated     Show installed packages that have a newer release
  info         Show details about a package, installed or not [alias: show]
  why          Explain how a package would be resolved, without installing it
  changelog    Show what changed: the package's own changelog, or its release notes
  search       Search GitHub for installable repositories
  history      Show what was installed, upgraded and removed, newest first
  stats        Summarise everything ketch has recorded
  update       Refresh the package registry (see `upgrade` for installed packages)
  upgrade      Upgrade installed packages to their latest release
  rollback     Switch a package back to a version still on disk
  prune        Remove retained prefixes according to the retention policy
  pin          Hold a package at its current version
  unpin        Release a pin
  link         Re-create the links for an installed package
  unlink       Remove the links for an installed package, keeping it installed
  lock         Write or check `ketch.lock`, a reproducible record of what is installed
  sync         Install everything `ketch.lock` names, at the versions it names
  doctor       Check the environment and the install tree
  config       Write a package config (`ketch.toml`) by answering questions
  registry     Compare a package config with the registry's copy before pulling-requesting it
  path         Put the ketch bin directory on PATH
  plugin       Manage source plugins
  self         Manage ketch itself
  completions  Print a shell completion script, or install it with `--install`

Options:
      --root <DIR>
          ketch root directory (default: ~/.ketch)

  -v, --verbose
          Show what ketch is doing, including every request

  -q, --quiet
          Only print errors and requested data

      --no-color
          Never emit ANSI colour

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

```
